{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1498113815,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyMzc3NDY0MTQ=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1600","origin":"github"},"title":"How much would it take to have no (semipermanent) GitPython Repo instances anymore?","message":"GitPython self-describes as \"leaky\", not suitable for long running processes. Despite that we have repeatedly RF'ed our code to _increase_ the lifetime of GitPython `Repo` instances.\n\nI would like to assess how much it would take to minimize the usage of these instances. Please contribute your concerns and insights!\n\n### Interim conclusion:\n\nIt seems viable to remove *ANY* usage of GitPython API outside outside our own Repo classes.\n\nMoreover, with lazy loading of GitPython Repo instances in-place, our own repo classes should try hard not to access `self.repo` as much as possible.\n\n### Usage of GitPython API (please help complete):\n\n- `crawler` uses GitPython API to manage commits and tags, and all kinds of other things\n- `crawler` tests use it for `diff`s\n- `remove` to locate submodules (gone with #1604)\n- `update` tests to query tags (gone in #1602)\n- `save` to ask for the lastest commit shasum (gone with #1605)\n- ~~`aggregate` to add a file to git~~  [false positive]\n- `Dataset` to get the gitdir path (gone in #1606), and to checkout a commit (rarely used, if ever)\n- ~~`get` to update submodules~~ [false positive]\n- `publish` to diff against the latest commit, check remote branch existance (gone in #1544)","files":null}]}